      "text": "To solve the given functional equation \\( f(x) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 4^x \\), we start by substituting \\( x \\) with \\( \\frac{1}{x} \\). This gives us:\n\n\\[ f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) - 2 f(x) = 4^{\\frac{1}{x}}. \\]\n\nNow we have a system of two equations:\n1. \\( f(x) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 4^x \\)\n2. \\( f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) - 2 f(x) = 4^{\\frac{1}{x}} \\).\n\nWe can solve this system by eliminating \\( f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) \\). First, let's multiply the first equation by 2:\n\n\\[ 2 f(x) - 4 f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 2 \\cdot 4^x. \\]\n\nNow we have:\n1. \\( 2 f(x) - 4 f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 2 \\cdot 4^x \\)\n2. \\( f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) - 2 f(x) = 4^{\\frac{1}{x}} \\).\n\nNext, we add these two equations together:\n\n\\[ (2 f(x) - 4 f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right)) + (f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) - 2 f(x)) = 2 \\cdot 4^x + 4^{\\frac{1}{x}}. \\]\n\nSimplifying the left-hand side, we get:\n\n\\[ 2 f(x) - 4 f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) + f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) - 2 f(x) = -3 f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right). \\]\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\n\\[ -3 f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 2 \\cdot 4^x + 4^{\\frac{1}{x}}. \\]\n\nSolving for \\( f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) \\), we get:\n\n\\[ f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = -\\frac{2 \\cdot 4^x + 4^{\\frac{1}{x}}}{3}. \\]\n\nNow, we substitute \\( f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) \\) back into the first equation. From the first equation, we have:\n\n\\[ f(x) - 2 f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = 4^x. \\]\n\nSubstituting \\( f \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) \\), we get:\n\n\\[ f(x) - 2 \\left( -\\frac{2 \\cdot 4^x + 4^{\\frac{1}{x}}}{3} \\right) = 4^x. \\]\n\nSimplifying the right-hand side, we get:\n\n\\[ f(x) + \\frac{4 \\cdot 4^x + 2 \\cdot 4^{\\frac{1}{x}}}{3} = 4^x. \\]\n\nCombining the terms, we get:\n\n\\[ f(x) + \\frac{4^{x+1} + 2 \\cdot 4^{\\frac{1}{x}}}{3} = 4^x. \\]\n\nMultiplying through by 3 to clear the fraction, we get:\n\n\\[ 3 f(x) + 4^{x+1} + 2 \\cdot 4^{\\frac{1}{x}} = 3 \\cdot 4^x. \\]\n\nSimplifying further, we get:\n\n\\[ 3 f(x) + 4^{x+1} + 2 \\cdot 4^{\\frac{1}{x}} = 3 \\cdot 4^x. \\]\n\n\\[ 3 f(x) + 4 \\cdot 4^x + 2 \\cdot 4^{\\frac{1}{x}} = 3 \\cdot 4^x. \\]\n\n\\[ 3 f(x) + 4 \\cdot 4^x + 2 \\cdot 4^{\\frac{1}{x}} = 3 \\cdot 4^x. \\]\n\n\\[ 3 f(x) = 3 \\cdot 4^x - 4 \\cdot 4^x - 2 \\cdot 4^{\\frac{1}{x}}. \\]\n\n\\[ 3 f(x) = 4^x - 4 \\cdot 4^x - 2 \\cdot 4^{\\frac{1}{x}}. \\]\n\n\\[ 3 f(x) = 4^x - 4 \\cdot 4^x - 2 \\cdot 4^{\\frac{1}{x}}. \\]\n\n\\[ 3 f(x) = -3 \\cdot 4^x - 2 \\cdot 4^{\\frac{1}{x}}. \\]\n\n\\[ f(x) = -\\frac{3 \\cdot 4^x + 2 \\cdot 4^{\\frac{1}{x}}}{3}. \\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\( f(2) \\):\n\n\\[ f(2) = -\\frac{3 \\cdot 4^2 + 2 \\cdot 4^{\\frac{1}{2}}}{3}. \\]\n\n\\[ f(2) = -\\frac{3 \\cdot 16 + 2 \\cdot 2}{3}. \\]\n\n\\[ f(2) = -\\frac{48 + 4}{3}. \\]\n\n\\[ f(2) = -\\frac{52}{3}. \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( f(2) \\) is \\(\\boxed{-\\frac{52}{3}}\\).",
